<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Walafrid Strabo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 808–<a href="/years/849">849</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Walafrid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> was a Frankish monk, poet and theologian, educated at Reichenau and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hrabanus-maurus">Rabanus Maurus</a>. He served as tutor to the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a> at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> and from 838 was abbot of Reichenau, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>. His works include the gardening poem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hortulus">Hortulus</a>, a Life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-gall">Saint Gall</a> and the Visio Wettini. He drowned crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> in 849.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walafrid-strabo.jpg" alt="Walafrid Strabo" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rijksmuseum · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Walahfrid_Strabo_Strabus_monachus_(titel_op_object)_Liber_Chronicarum_(serietitel),_RP-P-2016-49-86-3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
